@@ -75,3 +75,6 @@ Backend integration tests run the real API (`dotnet Competency.Api.dll` from the
 - Arrangements worth reusing: `MatrixKit`'s track is a draft (the admin creates it, nothing approves it), so the superior approver's read of it is testable without extra setup, and the matrix direction is always active (a matrix is created only on an active one), so a draft direction never reaches an approval route. A block written by the head above for the matrix unit is a draft; its read by the unit's head is the head-visible case. A scoped draft block outside the responsible HR's units: assign an HR on the team itself (units = the team), grant the team head the HR role after the head made a draft competency, let that head create the block for the department with the competency in it, and flip the responsible HR's roles between two identical accept requests as the control (409 NoRight, then 200).
 - A position linked to a draft track of one unit, listed on the department's list, shows the track name to that unit's head and hides it from a sibling head (refusal text of the position matrix and `linkedTrack` of `matrix-positions`); two blocker kinds in one publication preview (a draft direction whose name an active one took, plus a level with no position) exercise the per-type cache of the visibility filter.
 - Hidden blocker of a publication list: a matrix version's plan holds every draft level of its draft track, whether the version content names it or not. A level takes its track's status, and two draft levels of one name by different authors are both allowed on creation and both flagged `NameTaken` at publication (the plan flips every entry to active while it checks names). So the team head creates a level named like the admin's `Junior` in upper case, outside the content: the superior approver names the one in the content and gets `moreCount` 1 for the other, the responsible HR and the administrator get both. Preview is `GET matrix-versions/{id}/publication`; the refusal of a publishing step needs the responsible HR as author: grant the team head the HR role, `AssignHrAsync(team)`, create the kit with `author:` that member's client, submit, and the department head's approve answers 409. An HR role with no assignment (`GrantHrRoleAsync` on the department head) is the superior approver who is an HR outside the unit; `org.SiblingHr` is the HR with no route, for the control.
+- Hidden draft of a publication list, as the superior approver reads it: the responsible HR (team head granted the HR role, assigned to the team) authors the kit and adds a second draft level to the kit's track outside the version document, linked to a position and uniquely named so no blocker arises; after `submit` the department head's `approve` is a publishing action. Department head: that level unlisted, `hiddenEntryCount` 1; responsible HR and administrator: all four entries, 0; one `cascadeTag` for all three. An entry preview (`GET /{segment}/{id}/publication`) can never hide anything, because whoever may approve the root reads every draft of its cascade (unit-less drafts as any HR, levels as the track unit's HR): pin only `hiddenEntryCount = 0` there, and record the hidden case as unreachable rather than inventing an arrangement. "Nowhere in the body" is asserted by walking the parsed JSON for strings (`JsonNode.ToJsonString` escapes Cyrillic); a missing field reads as a sentinel (-1) so the pre-fix copy names an assertion instead of throwing, and `AssertionScope` lists every one.
+- Desk scenario pages (`DraftsQueuePage`, `ReviewPage`, `BuilderSubmitStep`) mount in vitest with `Providers`, `createMemoryRouter` and `fakeApi` on a handful of routes; unhandled routes answer 599 and only their own sections fail. A `MatrixVersion` fixture needs `entries.levels`, `entries.competencies` and `entries.scales` arrays or `useVersionNames` throws on `.map`. A disabled button exists before its data: `findByRole` on its plain name resolves early, so wait for the text that arrives with the data. A component whose prop was renamed by the fix goes red on the pre-fix copy with a TypeError, not on behaviour, so its proof is mutation; say so in the plan.
+- Close a `suite` timing op right after its run (`--close`): closing after authoring stretches it over work that is not the run.
